---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---

<Base
  title="Cue: Playwright rules and an app map for AI agents"
  description="Cue gives your AI agent your team's Playwright rules as ESLint rules, and a map of your app's real routes and selectors."
>
<main class="page" id="top">
  <!-- ======== Hero ======== -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="stack">
      <span class="label">For Playwright teams with AI agents</span>
      <h1>Your agent knows Playwright. <span class="soft">It doesn't know your app.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Cue gives it its cues: your team's testing rules as ESLint rules it can't talk past, and a
        map of the routes and selectors your application really has.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <div class="install">
          <code id="cmd-install">npx @wiluszdamian/cue-cli init</code>
          <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-install" aria-label="Copy install command"><svg class="i-copy"><use href="#i-copy" /></svg><svg class="i-check"><use href="#i-check" /></svg></button>
        </div>
        <button class="prompt-btn" type="button" data-copy="agent-line" aria-describedby="agent-line">
          <span class="agents-stack" aria-hidden="true">
            <span title="Claude"><svg><use href="#i-claude" /></svg></span>
            <span title="Codex"><svg><use href="#i-codex" /></svg></span>
            <span title="Cursor"><svg><use href="#i-cursor" /></svg></span>
          </span>
          <span class="btn-labels"><span class="idle-text">Copy prompt</span><span class="done-text" aria-live="polite"><svg><use href="#i-check" /></svg>Copied</span></span>
          <span class="prompt-peek" id="agent-line" role="tooltip">Set up Cue in this Playwright project: run npx @wiluszdamian/cue-cli init, show me the plan before writing anything, then run npx @wiluszdamian/cue-cli doctor and fix what it reports.</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-row">
        <span>Node 22.13+</span><span>MIT</span><span>Runs locally</span><a href={url('docs/start/install/')}>Quickstart →</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="term" id="hero-term" aria-label="Example terminal session: installing Cue, surveying a page, and ESLint blocking a hard wait">
      <div class="term-bar">
        <span class="term-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
        <span class="term-title">~/shop-e2e — zsh</span>
        <span class="term-side">
          <span class="cue-pill" id="cue-pill"><i></i><span id="cue-state">standby</span></span>
          <button type="button" id="replay" aria-label="Replay the terminal session">Replay</button>
        </span>
      </div>
      <pre class="term-body" id="hero-screen"><span class="t-prompt">❯</span> <span class="t-cmd">npx @wiluszdamian/cue-cli init</span>
<span class="t-dim">Detected: claude-code, cursor</span>
  <span class="t-ok">create</span>  AGENTS.md
  <span class="t-ok">create</span>  eslint.config.mjs
  <span class="t-ok">create</span>  .agent-kb/
  <span class="t-warn">skip</span>    playwright.config.ts <span class="t-dim">yours, left alone</span>
<span class="t-hl">?</span> Apply this plan? <span class="t-ok">yes</span>

<span class="t-prompt">❯</span> <span class="t-cmd">cue survey http://localhost:3000/login</span>
<span class="t-ok">✓</span> /login <span class="t-dim">4 elements recorded</span>
  button <span class="t-hl">getByRole('button', &#123; name: 'Log in' &#125;)</span>

<span class="t-prompt">❯</span> <span class="t-cmd">npx eslint tests/login.spec.ts</span>
<span class="t-errline"><span class="t-dim">14:3</span>  <span class="t-bad">error</span>  no-hard-waits</span>
  <span class="t-dim">waitForTimeout waits on the clock, not on the app.</span>
  <span class="t-dim">Use</span> <span class="t-ok">await expect(locator).toBeVisible()</span>
<span class="t-bad">✗ 1 problem</span> <span class="t-dim">· agent rewrites the wait</span> <span class="t-ok">✓ 0 problems</span></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== The gap ======== -->
  <section class="leaf" id="gap">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">Pencil note, rehearsal 1: the agent improvised.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>A reasonable test, for some other project.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Ask an agent for a Playwright test and it writes what it has seen a thousand times. It
        invents selectors your app doesn't have and skips the conventions your team agreed on,
        because it has never seen your repo or your application.
      </p>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="correction pending" data-reveal>
          <span class="label">Selector</span>
          <code class="wrong">page.locator('#btn-submit-2')</code>
          <code class="right">getByRole('button', &#123; name: 'Log in' &#125;)</code>
          <p class="why">From .agent-kb/, recorded on /login after actually looking.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="correction pending" data-reveal>
          <span class="label">Waiting</span>
          <code class="wrong">await page.waitForTimeout(3000)</code>
          <code class="right">await expect(alert).toBeVisible()</code>
          <p class="why">From no-hard-waits, enforced by ESLint on every save and push.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== Running order ======== -->
  <section class="leaf" id="running-order">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="cue-no">1–4</span>
      <span>The running order. Cues are called in this sequence.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>The running order</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Four cues from an untouched repo to a test your team would sign off. The last one runs in
        CI with no agent in the room.
      </p>
      <div class="sheet-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Cue</th>
              <th>Called when</th>
              <th>Command</th>
              <th>What happens</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="no">1</td>
              <td>Once per repo</td>
              <td><code>npx @wiluszdamian/cue-cli init</code></td>
              <td>Detects your agents, prints a plan and writes nothing until you say yes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="no">2</td>
              <td>Per route, when it changes</td>
              <td><code>cue survey &lt;url&gt;</code></td>
              <td>Records what is really on the page into <code>.agent-kb/</code>, with a date.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="no">3</td>
              <td>Whenever you want a test</td>
              <td>Ask your agent</td>
              <td>It reads the rules and the map on its own and looks selectors up instead of guessing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="no">4</td>
              <td>Every push</td>
              <td><code>eslint . &amp;&amp; cue verify --ci</code></td>
              <td>A broken rule fails the build. Map drift fails the check.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== Rules ======== -->
  <section class="leaf" id="rules">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">The book is law. A document is a suggestion; a red build is not.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>Rules your agent can't talk past</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Your conventions ship as real ESLint rules. Every message says what is wrong, why, and what
        to write instead, because "violates rule" teaches an agent to route around it and naming the
        replacement teaches it to fix the test.
      </p>
      <div class="term"><div class="term-bar"><span class="term-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="term-title">npx eslint tests/</span></div>
<pre class="term-body"><span class="t-prompt">❯</span> <span class="t-cmd">npx eslint tests/</span>

<span class="t-hl">tests/checkout.spec.ts</span>
<span class="t-errline">  <span class="t-dim">14:3</span>  <span class="t-bad">error</span>  no-hard-waits</span>

  waitForTimeout waits on the clock, not on the app. Wait on the
  state you actually care about with a web-first assertion, which
  retries until the condition holds: <span class="t-ok">await expect(locator).toBeVisible()</span>,
  <span class="t-ok">.toHaveText()</span>, <span class="t-ok">.toBeEnabled()</span>. For a network round-trip, use
  <span class="t-ok">await page.waitForResponse(...)</span>.

<span class="t-bad">✗ 1 problem</span></pre></div>
      <div class="stack" style="gap: 10px">
        <span class="label">Eleven rules, ten enforced automatically</span>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li><span>no-hard-waits</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>web-first-assertions</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>no-raw-selectors</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>no-locators-in-tests</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>strict-zod-objects</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>no-focused-tests</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>skips-need-a-reason</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>require-test-tags</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>no-explicit-any</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>no-hardcoded-urls</span><span>enforced</span></li>
          <li><span>selectors-from-agent-kb</span><span>review only</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p style="color: var(--pencil); font-size: var(--step--1)">
          They live in one YAML file. Change it and the ESLint rules, the docs and the agent
          instructions update together. <code>cue explain no-hard-waits</code> prints the reasoning
          for any rule.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== Map ======== -->
  <section class="leaf" id="map">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">Blocking notes: where everything actually stands on stage.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>A map of the app, with a date on every entry</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Cue writes down what is on each page after actually looking, and cross-checks it against
        your source. Every entry can answer one question: why does Cue believe this?
      </p>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="term"><div class="term-bar"><span class="term-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="term-title">cue locator</span></div>
<pre class="term-body"><span class="t-prompt">❯</span> <span class="t-cmd">cue locator "Log in" --route /login</span>

<span class="t-dim">Route:</span>      /login
<span class="t-dim">Locator:</span>    <span class="t-hl">getByRole('button', &#123; name: 'Log in' &#125;)</span>
<span class="t-dim">Test id:</span>    login-submit
<span class="t-dim">Confidence:</span> <span class="t-ok">confirmed</span>
            Present in the product source
            and seen running.
<span class="t-dim">Freshness:</span>  <span class="t-ok">fresh</span></pre></div>
        <div class="stack" style="gap: 14px; align-content: start">
          <p>
            <strong>Confidence</strong> says whether a locator was confirmed in source and at
            runtime, seen only at runtime, or found only in code. The agent knows which ones may
            move.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Unknown means unknown.</strong> With no entry, Cue says so and names the command
            that fills the gap. It never hands back a plausible guess.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Verify</strong> reports exactly what it checked against the running app:</p>
          <div class="verdicts">
            <span class="verdict v-pass">PASS</span>
            <span class="verdict v-other">PARTIAL</span>
            <span class="verdict v-other">NOT VERIFIED</span>
            <span class="verdict v-fail">FAIL</span>
            <span class="verdict v-other">EMPTY</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== Who decides ======== -->
  <section class="leaf" id="who">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">One voice calls the show. Everyone else takes the cue.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>Cue doesn't teach Playwright. It decides who does.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Your agent may load Microsoft's Playwright skills, Playwright MCP and a community reference
        skill. None of them knows the others exist. Cue installs them and adds one table that says
        which source wins on each topic.
      </p>
      <div class="sheet-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Topic</th>
              <th>Decided by</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Page objects, locator strategy, test structure, tagging</td>
              <td><code>cue</code> <span class="wins">wins</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Your app's selectors, routes and vocabulary</td>
              <td><code>agent-kb</code> <span class="wins">wins</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Running, debugging, tracing, reports</td>
              <td><code>playwright-official</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Single scripted browser actions</td>
              <td><code>playwright-mcp</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Accessibility, visual regression, Electron</td>
              <td><code>reference-skill</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <span class="label" style="margin-top: 14px">Three lookups over MCP, read-only</span>
      <ul class="tools">
        <li>
          <code>explain_rule</code>
          <span>Why a rule exists and what to write instead.<span class="ans">explain_rule("no-hard-waits")</span></span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>resolve_owner</code>
          <span>Which source wins on a topic.<span class="ans">resolve_owner("test data strategy") → cue</span></span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>resolve_locator</code>
          <span>A real selector, with confidence and freshness.<span class="ans">resolve_locator("Log in", "/login")</span></span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== House rules ======== -->
  <section class="leaf">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">House rules. Posted by the stage door.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>Your repo stays yours</h2>
      <ul class="house">
        <li>
          <h3>Running init twice changes nothing</h3>
          <p>Every write goes through a plan you can read first.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Your edits are never overwritten</h3>
          <p>A file you changed by hand is reported and left alone. Existing files are touched only inside a marked region.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Uninstall is exact</h3>
          <p>Removal reverses what was installed from a recorded manifest, not from guesswork.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== Not proven ======== -->
  <section class="leaf">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">Previews, not opening night.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body stack">
      <h2>Does it make agents write better tests? Not measured yet.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Cue includes a harness that records and scores agent runs. No benchmark has been run, so we
        don't claim better tests. What you can check today is the mechanism: rules that fail the
        build, and selectors verified against the running app. The numbers will go here when they
        exist, whatever they say.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== FAQ ======== -->
  <section class="leaf" id="faq">
    <aside class="margin">
      <span class="note">Questions from the house.</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need an AI agent to use it?</summary>
        <p>No. The ESLint rules work on their own, in CI and in your editor, and catch the same mistakes from people.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which agents does it set up?</summary>
        <p>Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Grok, Gemini CLI and OpenCode. Every agent reads <code>AGENTS.md</code>, so the baseline applies even to one Cue doesn't detect.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it work on an existing, messy suite?</summary>
        <p>That's the main target. Cue leaves files it doesn't own alone, and the map is useful long before the whole app is surveyed.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why the long package name?</summary>
        <p>Always install the scoped <code>@wiluszdamian/cue-cli</code>. An unscoped name run through <code>npx</code> can fetch an unrelated package. Once installed, the short <code>cue</code> command is enough.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does anything leave my machine?</summary>
        <p>No. The CLI, the ESLint plugin, the map and the MCP server all run locally. There is no hosted service.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======== Finale ======== -->
  <section class="finale">
    <span class="label">Final call</span>
    <h2>Places, please.</h2>
    <div class="install">
      <code id="cmd-install-2">npx @wiluszdamian/cue-cli init</code>
      <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-install-2" aria-label="Copy install command"><svg class="i-copy"><use href="#i-copy" /></svg><svg class="i-check"><use href="#i-check" /></svg></button>
    </div>
    <p style="color: var(--pencil)">A plan first. Nothing written until you say yes.</p>
  </section>
</main>
</Base>

<script>
  import { initCopyButtons } from '../lib/copy';
  import { initCorrections, initHeroTerminal } from '../lib/landing';

  initCopyButtons();
  initCorrections();
  initHeroTerminal();
</script>
